A closure captures the variable i, not its value at creation time. Python looks the name up when the lambda is called (late binding), and by then the loop has finished and i is 2 in the enclosing scope (a list comprehension has its own scope in Python 3, but all three lambdas share that one i cell). Hence [2, 2, 2].

Fixes: bind the current value at creation. A default argument is evaluated at definition time; functools.partial stores the value in the partial object; a factory function gives each closure its own scope with its own variable.

```python
from functools import partial

def _identity(x):
    return x

def make_default_arg(n):
    return [lambda i=i: i for i in range(n)]

def make_partial(n):
    return [partial(_identity, i) for i in range(n)]

def _make_getter(i):
    return lambda: i

def make_factory(n):
    return [_make_getter(i) for i in range(n)]
```

Downside of the default-argument trick: it changes the signature, so a caller (or a framework passing an argument, such as a GUI event) can override the bound value by accident, e.g. cb(99) returns 99. partial and the factory keep the callable's real signature clean; the factory is the most explicit and works for multi-line closures. Also note this is not specific to lambdas: nested def statements in a loop behave identically.

