Use iter() once, then pull chunks with itertools.islice, which consumes exactly n items from a shared iterator. For the window, keep a deque with maxlen=n so appending evicts the oldest item. To raise ValueError at call time, make the public function a normal function that validates and then returns an inner generator; a generator function would defer the check until the first next().

```python
from collections import deque
from itertools import islice

def batched(iterable, n):
    if n < 1:
        raise ValueError("n must be at least 1")
    def gen():
        it = iter(iterable)
        while True:
            batch = tuple(islice(it, n))
            if not batch:
                return
            yield batch
    return gen()

def sliding_window(iterable, n):
    if n < 1:
        raise ValueError("n must be at least 1")
    def gen():
        it = iter(iterable)
        window = deque(islice(it, n), maxlen=n)
        if len(window) < n:
            return
        yield tuple(window)
        for item in it:
            window.append(item)
            yield tuple(window)
    return gen()
```

Memory: both hold O(n) items at any moment regardless of input length, so they work on infinite streams or files too large for RAM. Doing `[data[i:i+n] for i in range(0, len(data), n)]` needs the whole sequence in memory first (and len/indexing, so it fails on generators). Pitfall: calling list(it) or islice on the original iterable twice would silently skip items; only one iterator must be shared. Python 3.12's itertools.batched returns tuples too, so these are drop-in for older versions.

