First collapse duplicates with groupby(...).agg(revenue=("revenue","sum")) (named aggregation). Compute share before filtering, using groupby("category")["revenue"].transform("sum"), which returns a Series aligned to the rows. For the filter: with a deterministic tie-break, sort by category, revenue desc, product asc and take groupby("category").head(2); with keep_ties, use rank(method="min", ascending=False) <= 2 so tied products share a rank.

```python
import pandas as pd

def top2_with_share(df, keep_ties=False):
    totals = df.groupby(["category", "product"], as_index=False).agg(
        revenue=("revenue", "sum")
    )
    totals["share"] = (
        totals["revenue"] / totals.groupby("category")["revenue"].transform("sum")
    )
    order = ["category", "revenue", "product"]
    asc = [True, False, True]
    if keep_ties:
        rank = totals.groupby("category")["revenue"].rank(method="min", ascending=False)
        kept = totals[rank <= 2]
    else:
        kept = totals.sort_values(order, ascending=asc).groupby("category").head(2)
    return kept.sort_values(order, ascending=asc).reset_index(drop=True)
```

Why this shape: transform keeps the original row count, so shares are computed against the full category before top-N filtering; computing them after head(2) would give shares that sum to 1 per category and be wrong. nlargest per group or head(2) after an unstable sort leaves ties to chance, which is why the tie-break column is explicit. rank(method="min") gives tied values the same lowest rank (1,2,2,4), so a three-way fight for second place returns all tied products. Pitfall: groupby.apply with a lambda is slower and its output index shape has changed across pandas versions, so prefer head/transform/rank. Floating-point shares should be compared with a tolerance.

