answer:
Hashing: add each item to a set and stop when an add does not grow the set; each add costs O(1) expected, so O(n) total, with O(n) extra memory. Sorting: sort a copy (O(n log n) comparisons), then equal items must be adjacent, so one pass over neighbouring pairs finds them; this needs only orderable items, not hashable ones.

```python
def has_duplicates_quadratic(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False

def has_duplicates(items):
    seen = set()
    for x in items:
        size = len(seen)
        seen.add(x)
        if len(seen) == size:
            return True
    return False

def has_duplicates_sorted(items):
    ordered = sorted(items)
    return any(a == b for a, b in zip(ordered, ordered[1:]))
```

Sorting beats hashing when items are unhashable but orderable (lists, or objects with __lt__ but no usable __hash__), when memory is tight (an in-place sort of data you may reorder needs O(1) extra space versus the set's O(n)), when hashing is expensive or adversarially collision-prone, or when you need the duplicates grouped anyway. Hashing wins on raw asymptotics for hashable data: expected O(n) versus O(n log n). Caveats: "O(n)" for sets is expected-case and assumes a good hash; the quadratic version has early exit, so its worst case is the all-unique input. Items equal by == must hash equally for the set approach to be right (e.g. 1 and 1.0 count as duplicates).

